title: a1.1 Garden walks, alone and in pairs
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Two activities, both walks: alone (SA-01) and with a fellow resident (SA-02). Fresh air and movement stimulate memory and steady emotions; company gives a reason to go. Breakdowns on walks alone: nobody to talk to, staff unavailable, confusion and attempts to go home. On pair walks: a slip the partner does not notice, a partner who does not fit, overexertion while talking. Stumbles while talking can happen on both.

= Situated Activity - A walk on your own [SA-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-01
|Name|A walk on your own
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* Forbes et al. (2015), Cochrane review: exercise may improve daily functioning in dementia
* Bratman et al. (2015): a walk in nature lowered rumination (healthy adults)
* Detweiler et al. (2008): an enclosed garden on a dementia unit; fewer agitated behaviours (one unit, before-after)
* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes is common
* Lai & Arthur (2003): wandering and exit-seeking in people with dementia
)))
|Goal|Go out when you like: fresh air, movement, a change of scene. Back safely.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - walks alone
* ST-04: Care worker - opens the door; notices who is out
* ST-06: Physiotherapist - assesses walking unaccompanied
)))
|Trigger|Restless; fine weather; garden door open; an old habit
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Decide to go
1. Find the door; coat, shoes, rollator
1. Walk the loop; look, listen
1. Rest on a bench
1. Notice when to turn back: tired, cold
1. Find the door; let staff know
)))
|Context|Only residents assessed fit (ST-06). Enclosed garden loop, one coded gate. Nobody to talk to; staff glance out of the window. The walk depends on staff: to open the door, keep an eye out, or walk along when walking alone is not allowed. Confused, some residents set out for a former home.
|Frequency and duration|Varies widely; 5-30 min; mostly in fine weather
|Tools and artifacts|Garden door, coded gate, loop path, benches, rollator, a window onto the garden
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Loop that leads back to the door
* Door open in fine weather; staff keep an eye out
* Accompanied walk when walking alone is not allowed
* Coded gate; staff redirect a resident heading for the exit
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Nobody to talk to: lonely; stops going out (PS-06)
* Staff unavailable: the door stays closed, nobody keeps an eye out, the walk does not happen
* Confused: forgets where or why; heads for the gate to go home
* Fall or stumble, unseen; tired, far from the door
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-04, ST-06); extended-by → SA-02; illustrated-by → PS-06; setting of → PS-02 (a consideration, not a breakdown).

= Situated Activity - A walk with a fellow resident [SA-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-02
|Name|A walk with a fellow resident
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* de Medeiros et al. (2012): residents with dementia form and keep friendships
* Orange et al. (1996): in Alzheimer's disease, trouble in conversation grows and repair succeeds less often
* Schegloff et al. (1977): speakers prefer to fix their own slips
* Bora et al. (2015), meta-analysis: theory of mind is impaired in Alzheimer's disease
* Clare (2003): people aware of decline protect themselves, or face it
* Lundin-Olsson et al. (1997): older people who stop walking when they talk fall more often
* Montero-Odasso et al. (2012): with cognitive impairment, walking slows and grows unsteady under a second task
)))
|Goal|Company as the reason to go out; a conversation both enjoy
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - walks, talks
* ST-02: Fellow resident - walks, talks
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - matches pairs
* ST-04: Care worker - starts the walk; keeps an eye out
)))
|Trigger|Staff see two residents who get along; one invites the other; a pair's habit
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Match: pace, mood, stage of dementia, who gets along with whom
1. Ask both
1. Introduce, or re-introduce
1. Walk side by side, at one pace
1. Talk: what they see, what they share
1. After a slip: give time; then the last topic, a new one, or the surroundings
1. Rest on a bench
1. One wants to go back: both turn
)))
|Context|The match makes or breaks the walk. Staff know some shared pasts, and who dislikes whom, informally. Names are forgotten. Slips grow more frequent as dementia progresses: a lost thread, a missing word, a story told twice, a belief from another time ("My mother is waiting"). Speakers prefer to repair their own slips; a partner with dementia often misses them. Many residents know they slipped, and feel it. Talking slows walking, hides tiredness and raises fall risk.
|Frequency and duration|Occasional; 15-30 min
|Tools and artifacts|Staff knowledge, care record, life-story book, garden, benches
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Pair residents who sit together at meals, or share a past
* Re-introduce them at each walk
* Same pair, same time, same route
* Care worker walks along
* Gentle redirection by staff: to the present, to the feeling, to a new topic
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* No partner; mismatch in pace or mood
* Two residents who dislike each other, paired by someone who did not know (PS-03)
* Agrees to please staff or the partner: "I suppose so" (PS-03)
* Loses the thread; the partner does not notice; the talk dies (PS-01)
* Helped too fast, corrected or quizzed ("You told me already", "Do you remember...?"): embarrassed
* After repeated slips: avoids the next walk
* A personal fact, used by staff to start a conversation, passed on (PS-05)
* Overexertion: distracted by the partner, walks past tiredness and the set time (PS-04)
* Stumbles while talking (PS-01, PS-04)
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01, ST-02 (supported by ST-04, ST-05); extends → SA-01; illustrated-by → PS-01, PS-03 to PS-05.

= The two walks compared =

|=Aspect|=SA-01 Alone|=SA-02 With a fellow resident
|Company|None|Present; often has dementia too
|Notices a slip|Nobody|The partner, often not
|Notices a stumble|Whoever looks out|The partner, if able
|Notices tiredness|The resident, if at all|Nobody: the talk is good

= References =

* Bora, E., Walterfang, M. & Velakoulis, D. (2015). Theory of mind in behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia and Alzheimer's disease: a meta-analysis. //Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery & Psychiatry//, 86(7), 714-719.
* Bratman, G. N., Hamilton, J. P., Hahn, K. S., Daily, G. C. & Gross, J. J. (2015). Nature experience reduces rumination and subgenual prefrontal cortex activation. //PNAS//, 112(28), 8567-8572.
* Clare, L. (2003). Managing threats to self: awareness in early stage Alzheimer's disease. //Social Science & Medicine//, 57(6), 1017-1029.
* Detweiler, M. B., Murphy, P. F., Myers, L. C. & Kim, K. Y. (2008). Does a wander garden influence inappropriate behaviors in dementia residents? //American Journal of Alzheimer's Disease & Other Dementias//, 23(1), 31-45.
* Forbes, D. et al. (2015). Exercise programs for people with dementia. //Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews//, (4), CD006489.
* James, I. A., Wood-Mitchell, A., Waterworth, A. M., Mackenzie, L. E. & Cunningham, J. (2006). Lying to people with dementia: developing ethical guidelines for care settings. //International Journal of Geriatric Psychiatry//, 21(8), 800-801.
* Lai, C. K. Y. & Arthur, D. G. (2003). Wandering behaviour in people with dementia. //Journal of Advanced Nursing//, 44(2), 173-182.
* Lundin-Olsson, L., Nyberg, L. & Gustafson, Y. (1997). "Stops walking when talking" as a predictor of falls in elderly people. //The Lancet//, 349(9052), 617.
* de Medeiros, K., Saunders, P. A., Doyle, P. J., Mosby, A. & Van Haitsma, K. (2012). Friendships among people with dementia in long-term care. //Dementia//, 11(3), 363-381.
* Mental Health Foundation (2016). //What Is Truth? An Inquiry about Truth and Lying in Dementia Care//. London: Mental Health Foundation.
* Montero-Odasso, M., Verghese, J., Beauchet, O. & Hausdorff, J. M. (2012). Gait and cognition: a complementary approach to understanding brain function and the risk of falling. //Journal of the American Geriatrics Society//, 60(11), 2127-2136.
* Orange, J. B., Lubinski, R. B. & Higginbotham, D. J. (1996). Conversational repair by individuals with dementia of the Alzheimer's type. //Journal of Speech and Hearing Research//, 39(4), 881-895.
* Schegloff, E. A., Jefferson, G. & Sacks, H. (1977). The preference for self-correction in the organization of repair in conversation. //Language//, 53(2), 361-382.
* Victor, C. R. (2012). Loneliness in care homes: a neglected area of research? //Aging Health//, 8(6), 637-646.
